  "generated_text": "To find the sum of the squares of the solutions to the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the solutions using the quadratic formula:**\n\n   The quadratic formula is given by:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n   \\]\n   For the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0\\), the coefficients are \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 4\\), and \\(c = -1\\).\n\n   Plugging these values into the quadratic formula:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{4^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-1)}}{2 \\cdot 2}\n   \\]\n   Simplify inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 8}}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{24}}{4}\n   \\]\n   Simplify \\(\\sqrt{24}\\) to \\(2\\sqrt{6}\\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{4}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the fraction:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4}{4} \\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = -1 \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the solutions are:\n   \\[\n   x_1 = -1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x_2 = -1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the sum of the squares of the solutions:**\n\n   We need to find \\(x_1^2 + x_2^2\\). First, express \\(x_1^2\\) and \\(x_2^2\\) in terms of \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\):\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = \\left(-1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = \\left(-1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n   Expand each square:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = \\left(-1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2 = (-1)^2 + 2(-1)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = 1 - \\sqrt{6} + \\frac{6}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = 1 - \\sqrt{6} + \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   Combine the constants:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = 1 + \\frac{3}{2} - \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = \\frac{2}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} - \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = \\frac{5}{2} - \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly,\n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = \\left(-1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2 = (-1)^2 + 2(-1)\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right) + \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = 1 + \\sqrt{6} + \\frac{6}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = 1 + \\sqrt{6} + \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   Combine the constants:\n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = 1 + \\frac{3}{2} + \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = \\frac{2}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} + \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = \\frac{5}{2} + \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sum the squares of the solutions:**\n\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = \\left(\\frac{5}{2} - \\sqrt{6}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{5}{2} + \\sqrt{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Combine the terms:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = \\frac{5}{2} + \\frac{5}{2} - \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = \\frac{5}{2} + \\frac{5}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = \\frac{10}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = 5\n   \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the squares of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
